================================================================================
i2c_evidence.log — IE520 I2C bus lockup -> master hard-reset during
                   `show tech-support`
================================================================================
Captured    : 2026-08-19 (NZST) on bench tb470, during the "link flapping when
              issuing show tech-support" test request.
Failing unit: IE520 stack member 1, S/N 264A23066, MAC 84e3.2787.0740,
              console /dev/u5 — Active Master in event #1, BACKUP in event #2.
Status      : REPRODUCED 2/2 — see section 6 for event #2, which pins the
              fault to member 1 the unit and narrows the trigger command.
Peer unit   : member 2, S/N 264A23052, MAC 84e3.2787.09c0, console /dev/u4.
Software    : IE520-tb470.rel = IE520-tomahawk_ie520-continuous.rel
              (sha256 09b2a5a3...33e8cf58, /tftpboot build of 2026-08-19)
Bootloader  : 9.1.0 (U-Boot 2025.01-04844-gd2292467da4d), booting from flash.
Uptime      : member 1 had been up 64 min 47 s at the lockup (booted 23:35:58Z).

Clock note  : device syslog is UTC; the tb470-side console logger stamps NZST
              (UTC+12). Both are given below where it matters.
              Timestamp precision: console-logger lines are stamped by the
              tb470-side reader (~±1 s of wall clock); device syslog lines are
              whole-second device time.

THE ONE-LINE CHAIN:
  `show tech-support` (77 s in) -> "i2c i2c-0: mv64xxx: I2C bus locked" on the
  master -> master's control plane wedges (TIPC keepalives stop ~3 s later) ->
  backup declares it unreachable and fails over (+10 s) -> wedged master
  HARD-RESETS 41 s after the lock (no shutdown sequence: watchdog-consistent) ->
  every link on that member drops = the "link flapping" the field report saw.

================================================================================
1. PRE-EVENT CONTEXT (what the box was doing before the trigger)
================================================================================
Topology at the time (all links carrying steady traffic, RSTP disabled on the
DUT, zero L2 loops by construction):
  port1.0.1 (m1, AT-SPTXc 1000BASE-T) <- 4050, vlan611, ~1000 pps each way
  sa2 = port1.0.9 (m1) + port2.0.1 (m2)  <- x230 LAG, vlan630, ~1000 pps each way
  port2.0.9 (m2, AT-SPTXc)  egress-only stream ~500 pps (vlan622 dead-end)
  port1.0.7 (m1, AT-SPSX fibre) -> port2.0.7 (m2), ~500 pps egress (vlan651/652)
Traffic profile was CONSTANT for >30 min before the event — no load change
anywhere near the lockup moment.

Timeline of everything member 1's console printed in the preceding 35 minutes
(from the persistent read-only logger on /dev/u5; NZST):

  12:05:09  port2.0.9/port1.0.9 forwarding      (static-channel legs restored)
  12:06:28  Pluggable[440]: Pluggable AT-SPSX inserted into port1.0.7   [NOTE A]
  12:06:33  Pluggable[397]: Pluggable AT-SPSX inserted into port2.0.7   [NOTE A]
  12:06:34  port2.0.7 forwarding
  12:06:35  port1.0.7 forwarding
  --- 34 minutes 10 seconds of TOTAL console silence (normal operation) ---
  12:40:45.8  i2c i2c-0: mv64xxx: I2C bus locked, block: 1, time_left: 0

[NOTE A] The Pluggable subsystem logged both fibre SFPs as freshly "inserted"
when their ports were MOVED BETWEEN VLANS (no one touched the hardware). So a
pure config action re-registers pluggables — i.e. touches the SFP/I2C
subsystem — 34 min before the lockup. Recorded for completeness; separated
from the lockup by a long quiet gap.

NEGATIVE CONTROL, 2.5 minutes before the trigger — DDM polling alone is fine:
  12:38:08 - 12:38:17 NZST: `show system pluggable diagnostics` issued 10x
  back-to-back from the same relayed console session. Every iteration completed
  in 1.0 s, all 3 DDM-capable modules (AT-SPTXc in 1.0.1, 1.0.9, 2.0.9) read
  successfully each time (Rx Up x3), zero log events, zero console output,
  max through-traffic gap during the burst 2.3 ms.
  => Simple per-SFP DDM reads DO NOT reproduce the lock. Whatever tech-support
  does differently (heavier EEPROM access, or I2C contention while the CPU is
  saturated by collection) is required.

SFP/I2C ARCHITECTURE (from member 1's own boot messages, 12:42:08):
  i2c i2c-0: Added multiplexed i2c bus 3 .. 30
  i2c-mux-gpmux sfp-i2c-mux: 28-port mux on mv64xxx_i2c adapter
  => ALL 28 SFP cages hang off ONE mv64xxx I2C controller through a 28-port
  mux. A single wedged mux transaction therefore blocks the only bus — there
  is no per-port isolation.

