7. BISECT RESULTS (2026-08-19 13:15-13:35 NZST) — NO SINGLE COMMAND REPRODUCES
================================================================================
Every candidate was executed ON MEMBER 1 (via `remote-login 1` from the master
session, so the commands ran member-locally exactly as tech-support's remote
battery does), with the read-only console logger armed and the i2c count
checked after every step. Stack healthy throughout; traffic running.

  CLEAN — single shot:
    show platform                              1.0 s
    show platform port counters                9.4 s
    show platform table internalports          1.0 s
  CLEAN — repetition (trio x10 = 30 executions, no settle)      all 1.0-9.5 s
  CLEAN — section-scoped tech-support (issued on the master):
    show tech-support system                   19.0 s
    show tech-support stack                    30.0 s
  CLEAN — I2C-adjacent commands, single shot on member 1:
    show system environment / environment counters
    show system fiber-monitoring
    show system pluggable detail               (full EEPROM read)
    show platform counter
    show system hardware-errors ddr
    show system usb
    show platform full-debug                   37.5 s, 300 KB
  CLEAN — sustained heavy load:
    show platform full-debug x5 back-to-back   ~160 s continuous, all ~32 s
  CLEAN — member-side spawned-session mechanic:
    remote-diff all show interface brief / mac address-table / ip route  x5

  LOCKS:  full `show tech-support` (all) — 2 for 2, at +77.2 s / +83.2 s.

INTERPRETATION. The fault is NOT a single bad command. Everything tech-support
runs (that is externally addressable) passes alone, repeated, and under
sustained load. Only the complete collection context reproduces — which points
at a RACE/CONTENTION on member 1's shared mv64xxx+mux I2C bus: the collection's
fresh-IMISH-session-per-command pattern plus concurrent master/member batteries
plus the kernel's periodic SFP poller colliding, with per-attempt probability
low (single shots pass) but ~80 s of full-battery hammering making a collision
near-certain. Consistent with both events landing at nearly the same offset
(+77/+83 s) and with member 2's immunity being a hardware or bootloader-init
difference rather than a software one.

MEMBER DIFFERENCES (the two units, from `show system` + pluggable inventory):
  identical: board AT-IE520-28GSX Rev X1-0 (ID 717), RAM 506728 kB, software.
  DIFFERENT:
    bootloader : member 1 = 9.1.0 OFFICIAL   member 2 = `pauld` DEV BUILD
                 (U-Boot does the low-level I2C controller/mux init — a real
                 candidate for why m2 never locks; ironic that the SUSPECT
                 unit runs the official loader)
    modules    : member 1 carries TWO DDM-capable AT-SPTXc (1.0.1 + 1.0.9);
                 member 2 carries ONE (2.0.9) — more DDM targets = more
                 kernel-poller I2C traffic on member 1.

NEXT LEVERS (beyond black-box CLI):
  - Swap test A: move member 1's AT-SPTXc modules to member 2 (or pull one of
    m1's) and re-run tech-support — discriminates module-population from unit.
  - Swap test B: flash the pauld bootloader onto member 1 (or 9.1.0 onto
    member 2) and re-run — discriminates bootloader I2C init.
  - Developer: instrument the mv64xxx driver / i2c-mux around the offload
    path; the reproducer is cheap (one command, ~80 s, 2/2 so far).

