9. RUN 3 — CLEAN SERIES ON THE HEALTHY UNIT + REBOOT-HISTORY CORRELATION
   (2026-08-19 14:00-14:15 NZST; member 1 POWERED OFF for the series)
================================================================================
Purpose: answer the ORIGINAL test request — does `show tech-support` flap
links on a healthy unit — fully de-confounded from the member-1 crash, and
with far wider SFP coverage.

Setup changes for this series:
  - Member 1 physically powered off (PDU) -> member 2 standalone Active Master.
  - Terrence populated member 2 to 17 SFP cages: adds 4x SFP LOOPBACK V2
    (2.0.6/2.0.8/2.0.14/2.0.16 — link-up), 4x AT-SP10SR, AT-SP10T, 2x AT-SPSX.
  - Loopback ports isolated one-VLAN-each (641-644) — REQUIRED with RSTP
    disabled: several loopbacks sharing a vlan re-multiply any flooded frame
    into a self-sustaining storm. Isolated, the reflection dies at the port.
  - Each loopback port given a routed egress stream (~500 pps) via static ARP
    to a fictitious next-hop, so all four carry live traffic whose interruption
    would be measurable. Through-path re-homed all-member-2:
    eth1 -> 4050 -> port2.0.9 (vlan611) -> routed -> sa2/port2.0.1 -> x230 -> eth2.
  - Detectors CALIBRATED with a deliberate shut/no-shut on port2.0.16: NSM
    logged both the down and up notification. (The 4050/x230 logs proved
    useless as witnesses — they do not record link transitions even for
    member-1's two hard resets; DUT NSM log + pcaps are the valid detectors.)

RESULT — `show tech-support` x3 on standalone member 2, 17 cages, ~4500 pps:
  iter 1: completed  65.1 s   iter 2: 73.1 s   iter 3: 55.0 s
  - i2c events ............................ 0
  - NSM link events during the runs ....... 0 (only my own sweep commands
    appear in the 02:04-02:08Z window)
  - worst traffic gap, any run, either direction ... 6.8 ms
    (72k-80k frames delivered per direction per run; loopback and egress
    streams uninterrupted)

VERDICT ON THE ORIGINAL TEST REQUEST:
  On this build, `show tech-support` does NOT flap links on a healthy IE520 —
  0 link events and <7 ms worst-case forwarding disturbance across 3 runs,
  17 populated cages, 5 concurrent traffic streams. Scoreboard for the whole
  investigation: 5 completed tech-support executions on member 2 (2 as stacked
  master, 3 standalone) — zero link events attributable to the command.
  The ONLY link impact ever observed is the member-1 i2c-lock -> watchdog
  reset, which drops every port on that member at once. "Link flapping when
  issuing show tech-support" is that crash seen from outside.
  Aggregation note: with cross-member LAGs the crash costs <1 s of traffic;
  with single-homed links it cost 186 s. Aggregated ports are NOT equally
  affected — they are the mitigation.

REBOOT-HISTORY CORRELATION (member 1, read after re-power; device UTC):
  02:08:21 Unexpected System reboot  = the PDU power-off/on by Terrence
                                       (power loss logs as the same generic
                                       string — NOT a crash)
  01:03:18 Unexpected System reboot  = i2c EVENT #2 (lock 01:01:01.6Z as
                                       backup; watchdog reset 01:01:43.6Z)
  00:42:58 Unexpected System reboot  = i2c EVENT #1 (lock 00:40:45.8Z as
                                       master; watchdog reset 00:41:26.9Z)
  23:35:58 Unexpected System reboot  = TIPC ejection #2 (Aug 18; 4 s after
                                       `no shutdown` port1.0.9 — 38385.log)
  23:16:38 Unexpected System reboot  = TIPC ejection #1 (Aug 18; seconds
                                       after member-1 config-update completed)
  20:30-20:36 two Unexpected         = bootloader netboot TFTP boot-loop era
  19:23:58 VCS duplicate master      = 17688-era resiliency-link defect
  (earlier Aug 17-18 entries: bootloader/build test churn incl. two
   "Bootup failed, rebooting." pairs)

TWO TAKEAWAYS FROM THE HISTORY:
  1. "Unexpected System reboot" is one generic label covering at least FOUR
     distinct causes on this unit (i2c-watchdog crash, TIPC-ejection reboot,
     power pull, netboot-loop restart). Reboot history alone cannot
     discriminate; it needs console/syslog correlation. No watchdog-specific
     reason string ever appears, so the watchdog mechanism is inferred from
     the 41/42 s lock-to-BootROM interval, not from this record.
  2. THE UNIT CONTRAST IS STARK: member 1 shows ~20 Unexpected reboots across
     Aug 17-19. Member 2's entire retained history is THREE entries, all
     "Expected User Request" — zero unexpected reboots ever. Whatever is
     wrong here lives in member 1.

EVENT-2 GAP CLOSED AS FAR AS POSSIBLE: member 1's permanent log holds NOTHING
between boot-up entries and the event-2 window (01:00-01:01Z empty) — its
remote battery hit no failing command before the lock, so the in-flight
command for event #2 remains unknowable from surviving records (contrast
event #1's BGP-battery trail).

