11. MODULE BISECT — HOW MANY / WHAT KIND OF SFPs ARE NEEDED
    (2026-08-19 15:55-16:30+ NZST; member 2 still dark; all runs solo member 1,
     driven on /dev/u5 with in-band i2c detection; modules returned to their
     ORIGINAL cages each step so module and port stay paired)
================================================================================
Protocol: per configuration, one 3-iteration `show tech-support` series
(a lock aborts the series; three cleans ~ 4 min). Inventory verified by
`show system pluggable` before every series.

  step 1  AT-SPTXc (DDM copper) alone in 1.0.1 ..... CLEAN 3/3 (62/53/53 s)
  step 2  AT-SPSX (non-DDM SX) alone in 1.0.7 ...... CLEAN 3/3 (53/53/52 s)
  step 3  TE stack PHY alone in 1.0.27 ............. CLEAN 3/3 (53/53/53 s)
  step 4  BOTH AT-SPTXc (1.0.1 + 1.0.9) ............ iter1 CLEAN 63 s
                                                     iter2 CLEAN 53 s
                                                     iter3 *** I2C LOCK at
                                                     +43.0 s *** -> watchdog
                                                     reset (EVENT #4)

EVENT #4 (lock epoch 1787112773.454 = 04:12:53Z): fourth reproduction, and
the most informative:
  - TWO DDM AT-SPTXc modules are SUFFICIENT. No SPSX, no stack PHYs, no
    stack, no traffic — just the two DDM coppers and the collection run.
  - FIRST DIRECT EVIDENCE THE TRIGGER IS PROBABILISTIC: two clean iterations
    preceded the lock. With 5 modules the lock always hit on iteration 1
    (3/3); with 2 modules it took 3 runs. Per-run lock probability scales
    with module count — exactly what the bus-collision model predicts.
  - Statistical caveat this introduces: 3 clean runs on a single module
    bounds its per-run probability loosely (p < ~0.5 at modest confidence);
    the singles are "not individually sufficient within 3 tries", not
    absolutely exonerated.

  step 5  non-DDM pair (SPSX 1.0.7 + PHY 1.0.27) ... CLEAN 6/6
          (71/82/53/52/52/52 s — DOUBLE the series length, respecting the
          probabilistic trigger; zero locks, zero reboots)

BISECT CONCLUSION — "how many / what kind":
  THE LOCK REQUIRES AT LEAST TWO DDM-CAPABLE MODULES on member 1's bus, and
  the per-run probability scales with (DDM) module count:
     0 modules ............ 0/4      (p ~= 0)
     any single module .... 0/3 each (p small; not individually sufficient
                                      within 3 tries)
     SPSX + stack PHY ..... 0/6      (p ~= 0 without DDM devices)
     2x DDM AT-SPTXc ...... 1st lock on run 3   (p ~= 1/3 per run)
     full population ...... locked run 1, 3/3   (p ~= 1 by +80 s)
  MECHANISM READ: the kernel's periodic DDM poller generates ongoing I2C
  traffic only to DDM-capable modules. With >=2 such modules, poller
  transactions interleave across mux channels and collide with
  tech-support's burst reads — and member 1's mv64xxx/mux path loses the
  race in a way member 2's does not (member 2 ran the same battery clean
  5x, including runs with a DDM SPTXc + 16 other modules fitted).
  UNTESTED REFINEMENT: a mixed pair (1 DDM + 1 non-DDM) would separate
  "two DDM required" from "one DDM plus any second bus occupant".

