NONE of these read the SFP i2c bus. Uncertainty is +-3-4 s, so the
leading named candidate is command 141 "show platform port" (the one
command in the window that plausibly touches port/module hardware state);
it is under direct loop test.

PHYSICAL DEFECT SIGNATURE FOUND IN THE MODULE'S OWN DDM:
  port2.0.9  AT-SPTXc ...102 (healthy, 221020):  Vcc 3.3185 V   normal
  port2.0.2  AT-SPTXc ...006 (SUSPECT, 210809):  Vcc 3.4167 V   *** High
                                          warning, threshold 3.4000 ***
The faulty module runs its supply rail ~100 mV high enough to trip the
DDM warning. Both report Tx Bias 131.07 / powers 6.5535 (0xFFFF saturated
- normal for copper SPTXc). This is an objective screening signature: a
field engineer can flag this module with "show system pluggable
diagnostics" without reproducing the crash.
Fibre-monitoring is NOT configured on any port ("show system
fiber-monitoring" empty), so it is not the polling agent.

WORKING CONCLUSION (to be confirmed by the loop test): no single
tech-support command reads the bus at the moment it wedges. The battery
supplies sustained CPU/bus contention; the actual i2c actor at the wedge
instant is the background pluggable/DDM poller transacting with the
marginal module. That is consistent with the earlier command-level bisect
finding that NO single command reproduces the lock standalone.

